* sg13g2-bandgap -- bandgap_core + bandgap_amp + bandgap_startup
* closed-loop AC power-supply-rejection (PSRR) testbench (issue #88,
* follow-on to #86/#58). Generated from
* sim/closed-loop-psrr/testbench/tb_closed_loop_psrr.spice.tmpl
* by sim/closed-loop-psrr/run_pvt_sweep.sh -- do not edit a generated
* netlist-snapshots/*.spice file directly, edit this template and re-run
* the sweep. Full methodology rationale is in
* sim/closed-loop-psrr/README.md.
*
* Generated for: corner=typ temp=125C vdd=3.30V
* DUT git sha (design/netlist/bandgap_core.spice,
* design/netlist/bandgap_amp.spice and design/netlist/bandgap_startup.spice
* this was copied from):
*   core=9530863 amp=397fe2d startup=66ac99d seeds=20261001-083332-e5507b2.csv@
* PDK: ihp-sg13g2 /home/ubuntu/share/pdk (see sim/pdk.json for the pinned release)
* OSDI models: /home/ubuntu/share/pdk/ihp-sg13g2/libs.tech/ngspice/osdi (built by sim/tools/build-osdi.sh)
*
* ------------------------------------------------------- injection method
* Unlike sim/loop-gain-phase-margin/, this testbench does NOT break the
* "fb" node -- the loop stays fully closed, exactly as
* sim/closed-loop-startup/ and sim/closed-loop-vref-pvt/ wire it (single
* shared fb node). PSRR is a closed-loop property by definition (how well
* the ALREADY-CLOSED loop rejects a supply perturbation at its output), so
* there is no loop-break analogous to the Middlebrook probe
* loop-gain-phase-margin needs to measure gain/phase around the loop.
* Instead, a small-signal AC perturbation is injected directly in series
* with Vvdd (Vvdd's own "ac 1 0" term below) -- the supply IS the
* stimulus -- while Vvss (the analog ground reference every node in this
* circuit, including vref, is ultimately measured against) stays fixed at
* its own separate DC source, unperturbed. This mirrors the textbook PSRR
* setup: perturb the rail being characterized, hold the reference rail
* still, read the resulting ripple at the node under test (vref).
*
* PSRR (this testbench's own sign/units convention, derived not assumed,
* see README "Sign convention"): with Vvdd's AC magnitude = 1 V (0 dB),
* PSRR(f) = 20*log10(|v(vdd,ac)| / |v(vref,ac)|) = -dB(v(vref)) directly
* (ngspice's db() is already 20*log10(|.|), and the 1 V AC stimulus is
* itself 0 dB) -- a LARGER PSRR_dB means BETTER rejection (less of the
* injected supply ripple reaches vref), the conventional PSRR sense.
*
* ------------------------------------------------------- DC bias: nodeset
* Same numerical situation sim/loop-gain-phase-margin/ documents in full:
* this circuit has (at least) two DC equilibria, and a bare `.op` with no
* transient bring-up and no bias hint has no guaranteed way to land in the
* intended closed-loop basin. Unlike that experiment, this testbench does
* NOT need to invent its own bring-up workaround -- the loop here is NOT
* broken (single "fb" node, exactly sim/closed-loop-startup/'s own
* topology), so nothing here disrupts fb's own fast dynamics the way
* splitting fb into fb_src/fb_load did there. The same fix is used anyway,
* for the same reason: seed a `.nodeset` at fb/sns1/sns2/vref from
* sim/closed-loop-startup's own most recent committed per-corner converged
* transient endpoint (read from that experiment's own record CSV by
* run_pvt_sweep.sh -- see README "Nodeset provenance"), which is (very
* nearly) the true closed-loop answer for this exact corner, so `.op`'s
* Newton-Raphson converges directly to it rather than searching from a
* default guess that might land in the degenerate all-off equilibrium
* design/bandgap_startup.sch exists to avoid.
.nodeset v(fb)=2.55589e+00 v(sns1)=5.89937e-01 v(sns2)=5.89325e-01 v(vref)=1.04218e+00

* DUT REFRESH (issue #264) -- the core DUT below is the TRIM-BEARING
* bandgap_core: R1 is segmented to its base l=37.2u into node tn0, and
* XXTRIM (the 255-unit binary-weighted rppd trim ladder landed by issue
* #229 -- see design/bandgap_trim_network.md) continues tn0 -> cb3. The
* ladder subcircuit below is copied device-for-device from
* design/netlist/bandgap_trim.spice, with its subcircuit-local
* .param trim_code pinned to the schematic default 128. This bench has NO
* trim-code axis (the code-swept benches are sim/trim-coverage/,
* sim/closed-loop-vref-trim-mc/ and sim/closed-loop-vref-boxtc-trim/);
* code 128 reproduces the pre-trim 511 um summing resistor to within ~3
* ohm, so this record's claim is the same claim the superseded pre-trim
* record made, re-measured against today's device set.

.lib "/home/ubuntu/share/pdk/ihp-sg13g2/libs.tech/ngspice/models/cornerHBT.lib" hbt_typ
.lib "/home/ubuntu/share/pdk/ihp-sg13g2/libs.tech/ngspice/models/cornerMOShv.lib" mos_tt
.lib "/home/ubuntu/share/pdk/ihp-sg13g2/libs.tech/ngspice/models/cornerRES.lib" res_typ

.options temp=125 tnom=27

* FIXTURE -- vdd carries BOTH the fixed DC operating point (matching the
* .nodeset seed above) AND the 1V/0deg small-signal AC stimulus this
* testbench measures rejection of. Vvss is a separate, unperturbed 0V
* source -- the ground reference vref is measured against -- so the AC
* stimulus genuinely represents supply ripple relative to a quiet ground,
* not a common shift of the whole circuit's reference.
Vvdd vdd 0 dc 3.30 ac 1 0
Vvss vss 0 dc 0
Vsub sub! vss dc 0

* DUT -- design/netlist/bandgap_core.spice, verbatim (loop NOT broken). M2/M3
* decomposed into per-leg-distinct counts (1/2/3) of dominant-plus-trim-
* finger sg13_hv_pmos instances (issue #149, T1 tracker #4 item 4 cause (d)
* -- breaks the M1/M2/M3 LVS graph automorphism at the schematic level;
* each branch's total mirror W/L stays 10u/1u nominally -- see
* sim/closed-loop-vref-pvt/testbench/tb_closed_loop_vref.spice.tmpl's own
* header for the fuller rationale and the {1,2,3} split's not-quite-exact
* electrical caveat, not repeated here).
XM1 sns1 fb vdd vdd sg13_hv_pmos w=10u l=1u ng=1 m=1
XQ1 sns1 sns1 vss vss npn13G2 Nx=1
XM2A sns2 fb vdd vdd sg13_hv_pmos w=9u l=1u ng=1 m=1
XM2B sns2 fb vdd vdd sg13_hv_pmos w=1u l=1u ng=1 m=1
XR2 sns2 cb2 sub! rppd w=2u l=82.7u m=1 b=0
XQ2 cb2 cb2 vss vss npn13G2 Nx=8
XM3A vref fb vdd vdd sg13_hv_pmos w=8u l=1u ng=1 m=1
XM3B vref fb vdd vdd sg13_hv_pmos w=1u l=1u ng=1 m=1
XM3C vref fb vdd vdd sg13_hv_pmos w=1u l=1u ng=1 m=1
XR1 vref tn0 sub! rppd w=2u l=37.2u m=1 b=0
XXTRIM tn0 cb3 sub! bandgap_trim
XQ3 cb3 cb3 vss vss npn13G2 Nx=1

.subckt bandgap_trim in out sub
*.iopin in
*.iopin out
*.iopin sub
XRU1 in t001 sub rppd w=2u l=3.43u m=1 b=0
